    <h1>Pregnancy Test Timing: When to Test</h1>

    <p>Taking a pregnancy test at the right time can mean the difference between an accurate result and a false negative. This guide explains how pregnancy tests work, when to test, and what to do with your result.</p>

    <h2>How Pregnancy Tests Work</h2>

    <p>Home pregnancy tests detect the hormone <strong>human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)</strong> in your urine. hCG is produced by the placenta after implantation.</p>

    <p>Most home tests can detect hCG at levels of 25 mIU/mL or lower, and some early detection tests at 10 mIU/mL, as stated on the test packaging. Sensitivity varies between brands. A more sensitive test needs less hCG in the urine to show a positive result. If you are not sure which test to use, a pharmacist can help you choose.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/</em></p>

    <h3>The hCG detection timeline</h3>

    <p>hCG starts to be produced around 6 days after fertilisation. Implantation is when the fertilised egg attaches to the lining of the womb, and the placenta begins to form at that point. hCG is present in the body only during pregnancy.</p>

    <p>Early pregnancy symptoms usually start when you are around 4 to 6 weeks pregnant, counted from the first day of your last period. A bleed similar to a very light period, around the time your period is due, can be implantation bleeding.</p>

    <p>In early pregnancy the amount of hCG in blood and urine rises quickly, doubling every 2 to 3 days. A test taken a day or two after a missed period is therefore more likely to find hCG than a test taken earlier.</p>

    <p>Ovulation can change from month to month, and a fertilised egg can implant at different times. The day on which hCG becomes detectable varies from person to person, and irregular cycles make it harder to work out when a period should start.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/signs-and-symptoms-that-might-mean-youre-pregnant/; Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="925728676" format="auto" />
    <h2>When to Take a Pregnancy Test</h2>

    <h3>Best time: after a missed period</h3>
    <p>For the most accurate result, take a pregnancy test <strong>after your period is late</strong>. Pregnancy tests are most reliable from the first day of your missed period. By this time, hCG levels are usually high enough to detect.</p>

    <p>If you do not know when your next period is due, do the test at least 21 days after you last had unprotected sex.</p>

    <p>Home tests often claim to be 99% accurate, but accuracy depends on how recently a period was missed and on the sensitivity of the test.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/; Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940</em></p>

    <h3>Early testing</h3>
    <p>Some tests are marketed as detecting pregnancy up to 6 days before a missed period, and some very sensitive tests can be used even before you miss a period. Testing this early increases the risk of a false negative because hCG levels may not yet be high enough.</p>

    <p>If you test early and get a negative result, wait a few days and test again if your period has not started.</p>

    <h3>Best time of day</h3>
    <p>Use your <strong>first morning urine</strong>, which is most concentrated and contains the highest hCG levels. Urine is most concentrated when you first get up, which makes hCG easier to find. If you test later in the day, avoid drinking large amounts of fluids before testing. NHS guidance notes that a test can be done on urine collected at any time of the day and does not have to be in the morning.</p>

    <p>Follow the instructions for how long to wait before checking the result, usually two minutes or more, and set a timer. Reading the result later than the time given in the instructions can give a misleading result. Most tests have a control indicator, and if that line or symbol is not visible the test is not working. Check the expiry date before you use a test.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/</em></p>

    <h2>What Can Affect Test Results</h2>

    <h3>False Negatives</h3>
    <p>A false negative (test says you are not pregnant, but you are) can happen if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You test too early</li>
      <li>Your urine is too diluted</li>
      <li>You do not follow the test instructions</li>
      <li>The test is expired or damaged</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A negative result is less reliable than a positive one. You are more likely to get a false negative if you take the test too soon, if you check the result before it is ready, or if you test later in the day on diluted urine. Not following the instructions properly also makes a result unreliable.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/; Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940</em></p>

    <h3>False Positives</h3>
    <p>A false positive (test says you are pregnant, but you are not) is rare but can happen if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You have recently been pregnant (hCG can remain in your body for weeks after a miscarriage or birth)</li>
      <li>You are taking fertility medications containing hCG</li>
      <li>You have a medical condition that produces hCG (rare)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>A false positive can also follow a pregnancy loss soon after the fertilised egg attached to the lining of the womb, and problems with the ovaries and the menopause may lead to a false positive. A faint line that appears after the time window given in the instructions is not a result. Read the result only within the time the instructions give.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/</em></p>

    <AdUnit slot="2263226824" format="auto" />
    <h2>What to Do with Your Result</h2>

    <h3>If the Test Is Positive</h3>
    <p>A positive result is almost always accurate. Contact your healthcare provider to schedule your first prenatal visit. Start taking a prenatal vitamin with folic acid if you are not already.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/</em></p>

    <h3>If the Test Is Negative</h3>
    <p>If your period is late and the test is negative:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Wait a few days and test again</li>
      <li>Use your first morning urine</li>
      <li>If your period is more than a week late, contact your healthcare provider</li>
    </ul>

    <p>If a test is negative and your period does not begin, take the test again in a few days or in one week. This matters most if you tested before or just after a missed period. If a second test is also negative and your period still has not arrived, speak to your GP. You may need a blood test.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: Mayo Clinic, https://www.mayoclinic.org/healthy-lifestyle/getting-pregnant/in-depth/home-pregnancy-tests/art-20047940; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/trying-for-a-baby/doing-a-pregnancy-test/</em></p>

    <h2>When to Call Your Clinician</h2>

    <p>Contact your healthcare provider if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You have a positive pregnancy test</li>
      <li>Your period is more than a week late and tests are negative</li>
      <li>You have irregular cycles and are unsure when to test</li>
      <li>You have symptoms of pregnancy but negative tests</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Before a first midwife appointment, contact your GP surgery or early pregnancy unit.</p>

    <h3>Warning signs of an ectopic pregnancy</h3>

    <p>An ectopic pregnancy is when a fertilised egg implants itself outside the womb, usually in one of the fallopian tubes. Around 1 in every 90 pregnancies in the UK is ectopic. Symptoms usually develop between the 4th and 12th week of pregnancy.</p>

    <p>Contact your GP or call NHS 111 if you have a combination of these symptoms and you might be pregnant, even if you have not had a positive pregnancy test: a missed period and other signs of pregnancy, tummy pain low down on one side, vaginal bleeding or a brown watery discharge, pain in the tip of your shoulder, or discomfort when peeing or pooing.</p>

    <p>Call 999 or go to your nearest accident and emergency department immediately if you have a sharp, sudden and intense pain in your tummy, feel very dizzy or faint, feel sick, or look very pale. These symptoms can mean the fallopian tube has split open, which is a medical emergency.</p>

    <p>Do not wait for a positive test before seeking help if you have these symptoms. Contact your clinician and give the date of your last period.</p>

    <p><em>Sources: NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ectopic-pregnancy/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/ectopic-pregnancy/symptoms/; NHS, https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/common-symptoms/pregnancy-symptoms-you-need-to-get-help-for/</em></p>

